### 2026-09-19 · 023 · Letter rendered upside down on Shade's machine — mechanism removed, trigger not reproduced
AGENT     claude-opus-5 (session c78670a9)
STATE     done — AWAITING CONFIRMATION on Shade's browser
TOUCHED   web/lib/pdf.ts, web/components/PdfCanvas.tsx
VERIFY    Rewrite verified in Chromium (dpr 1/1.5/2, throttled resize jitter,
          single mid-render resize at 20-80x CPU throttle, next dev Strict
          Mode), WebKit (dpr 1/2) and Firefox (dpr 1/2). Regression audit: 0
          network requests on selection, widths 900-1366 OK, print 2/2 pages.
SUMMARY   Shade's screenshot showed page 1 upside down, at one device pixel per
          PDF point, on a transparent background. Measured against the PDF, the
          letterhead sat at x=72, y=712 device px — raw PDF coordinates. In
          pdf.js the white background fill, the y-flip and the scale are all set
          in one step at the start of a render, and the only thing that undoes
          all three at once is the canvas being resized after that step.
          The old code could do exactly that: renders were never cancelled, and
          a re-render began by setting canvas.width on the canvas already on
          screen. Each render now draws into a brand-new canvas that is swapped
          in only when complete; superseded renders are cancelled and
          discarded. The DPR is folded into the viewport scale and only
          { canvas, viewport } is passed, instead of canvas + canvasContext +
          the separate `transform` option.
ACTIVE    none
NEXT      Shade: retest on the machine that showed it. If it recurs, the
          browser + version and whether it was Vercel or localhost are what's
          needed — see NOTES.
NOTES     HONEST STATUS: the OLD code rendered correctly in every condition I
          could construct, in all three engines. So the trigger on Shade's
          machine is unidentified; the rewrite removes the mechanism the
          screenshot proves, not a reproduced trigger. Candidates not testable
          here: a specific browser version, a canvas-touching extension (dark
          mode / privacy), GPU canvas acceleration.
          Testing gotcha: `next dev` in Next 16 rejects dev connections from
          127.0.0.1 when served as localhost — use http://localhost:PORT, or
          the page never hydrates and shows "No offer loaded".
